'''2.''' A mark or an impression left by the foot, whether of man or beast; a footprint; specifically, in ''paleon.'', an ichnite or ichnolite; a fossil footprint, or cast of an extinct animal's foot. Compare ''trace''<sup>1</sup>, 1, and ''trail''<sup>1</sup>, 2.

{{smaller block|Consider the atmosphere, and the exteriour frame and face of the globe, if we may find any ''tracks'' and footsteps of wisdom in the constitution of them.
{{right|''Bentley'', Works, I. viii. § 8.}}}}

'''3.''' A road; a path; a trail.

{{smaller block|Behold Torquatus the same ''track'' pursue.
{{right|''Dryden'', Æneid, vi. 1130.}}}}

{{smaller block|Up through that wood behind the church
There leads from Edward's door
A mossy ''track'', all over-boughed
For half a mile or more. ''Coleridge'', Three Graves.}}

{{smaller block|We all shrink, like cowards, from new duties, new responsibilities. We do not venture to go out of the beaten ''track'' of our daily life. ''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, p. 340.}}

'''4.''' A course followed; a way of going or proceeding: as, the ''track'' of a comet.

{{smaller block|Thy Fancy like a Flame its way does make,
And leaves bright ''Tracks'' for following Pens to take.
{{right|''Cowley'', To Sir W. Davenant.}}}}

{{smaller block|If straight thy ''track'', or if oblique,
Thou know'st not. ''Tennyson'', Two Voices.}}

'''5.''' The course or path laid out for horse-, foot-, bicycle-, or other races: as, a cinder ''track''; a ''track'' of six laps to the mile.—'''6.''' The two continuous lines of rails on which railway-cars run, forming, together with the ties, ballast, switches, etc., an essential part of the permanent way: as, a single ''track''; a double ''track''; to cross the ''track''. See cut under ''switch''.—'''7.''' In

readings.c3s1:
''anat.'', the course of a vessel, nerve, duct, etc.—8. In ''zoöl.'', the sole of the foot.—'''Double-track road''', {{smaller|a railroad having two tracks, so that trains may run in both directions at the same time.}}—'''In one's tracks''', {{smaller|where one stands; as one goes; hence, then and there; on the spot.}}

{{smaller block|He was in for stealing horses, but I think the real thief swore it off on him. If he did, God forgive him; he had better have shot the boy ''in his tracks.''
{{right|''The Century'', XL. 224.}}}}

'''Off the track''', {{smaller|thrown from the track; derailed, as a railway-carriage; colloquially, having wandered away from the subject under discussion: as, the speaker was a long way ''off the track.''}}—'''Side track.''' {{smaller|See ''side-track.''}}—'''Single-track road''', {{smaller|a railroad having only one track, but provided with turnouts at intervals, so that trains may run both ways.}}—'''To have the inside track.''' {{smaller|See ''inside.''}}—'''To make tracks''', {{smaller|to go away; quit; leave; depart. [Slang.]}}

{{smaller block|You will be pleased to ''make tracks'', and vanish out of these parts forever! {{right|''Kingsley'', Two Years Ago, xiv.}}}}

'''To make tracks for''', {{smaller|to go for; go after. [Slang.]}}

{{smaller block|"I ''made tracks for'' that lad," said Robert, . . . "I found him in the fields one morning."
{{right|''Mrs. Humphry Ward'', Robert Elsmere, xiii.}}}}

'''Track-laying machine''', {{smaller|a machine for laying rails in position on a railroad-track, the machine moving forward over each part of the track so laid.}}=''Syn.'' 3-6. ''Road, Path'', etc. (see ''way''), trail, pathway.

'''track'''<sup>2</sup> (trak), ''v. t.'' [< ''track''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.'' Cf. OF. ''tracquer'', surround in hunting, hunt down. In def. 3, cf. ''track''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', draw, from which, or its source, ''track''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.'' and ''v.'', is derived.] 1. To follow up the tracks of; follow by the tracks or traces left by that which is followed; trace; trail.

{{smaller block|It was often found impossible to ''track'' the robbers to their retreats. {{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., iii.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Through camp and town and wilderness
He ''tracked'' his victim. {{right|''Whittier'', Mogg Megone, ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|I will ''track'' this vermin to their earths.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}

'''2.''' To ascertain by means of existing traces or remains; trace.

{{smaller block|The whole line of their retreat might be ''tracked'' by the corpses of thousands who had died of cold, fatigue, and hunger. {{right|''Macaulay'', Frederic the Great.}}}}

'''3.''' To trace, follow, or mark out plainly.

{{smaller block|The straight course to her desire was ''tracked''.
{{right|''Drayton'', Barons' Wars, i. 32.}}}}

{{smaller block|A thirst to spend our fire and restless force
In ''tracking'' out our true, original course.
{{right|''M. Arnold'', The Buried Life.}}}}

'''4.''' To make tracks over; traverse: as, to ''track'' the desert.—'''5.''' To make marks upon, as with wet or muddy feet.

{{smaller block|"Stand still there!" she called to me as I approached the door, "and don't come in to ''track'' my floor."
{{right|''H. B. Stowe'', Oldtown, p. 21.}}}}

'''track'''<sup>3</sup>† (trak), ''n.'' [An erroneous form of ''tract''<sup>1</sup>, as ''tract''<sup>4</sup> is an erroneous form of ''track''<sup>2</sup>.] A tract of land.

{{smaller block|Those small ''tracks'' of ground, the county of Poole, and the like. {{right|''Fuller'', General Worthies. (''Richardson.'')}}}}

'''trackage'''<sup>1</sup> (trak′ạ̄j), ''n.'' [< ''track''<sup>1</sup> + ''-age''.] A drawing or towing, as of a boat on a river or canal; haulage; towage.

'''trackage'''<sup>2</sup> (trak′ạ̄j), ''n.'' [< ''track''<sup>2</sup> + ''-age''.] The collective tracks of a railway. ''Science'', XII. 46.

'''track-athletics''' (trak′ath-let″iks), ''n.'' Athletics which are conducted on a track, as running, hurdling, walking, and bicycling: sometimes used in an enlarged sense to include jumping,
